# Causal ABA Learning theory constraints

This repository investigates Causal ABA Learning.

Treat these files as the canonical project theory:
- `docs/theory/background.tex`
- `docs/theory/literature_review.tex`
- `docs/theory/theory_primer.md`
- `docs/theory/paper_summaries/`

Use PDFs in `docs/papers/core/` as reference material only. Read the summaries and theory files first.

Do not invent new theory. If a design choice is underspecified, ask the user or mark it explicitly as an assumption.

Core distinction:
- Causal ABA represents causal-discovery evidence inside ABA. Stable extensions correspond to compatible causal graph hypotheses.
- ABA Learning transforms or extends ABA frameworks from background knowledge and positive/negative examples.
- The project investigates how these can interact. Do not assume in advance that the system must learn only graphs, only rules, only facts, or only assumptions.

When working on experiments:
- distinguish qualitative toy-case analysis from quantitative benchmarking;
- preserve the link between stable extensions and candidate graph hypotheses;
- preserve the distinction between assumptions, rules, facts, constraints, contraries, attacks, and extensions;
- do not treat rejection of an independence assumption as automatically equivalent to dependence unless the formal construction supports it.

Your role is to help implement, test, inspect, and refactor code. The user makes the theoretical decisions.